window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.3","title":"The Central Limit Theorem","goal":"Show that sample means from a very skewed population become more symmetric and bell-shaped as the sample size grows from 1 to 5 to 30.","question":"A clinic manager knows waiting times are very skewed: mean 10 minutes, SD 10, most people wait briefly and a few wait very long. An audit of 40 patients found a mean wait of 12 minutes. Is 12 unusual, and can we use a bell curve at all for averages of skewed data?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A skewed population","text":"Waiting times in a clinic: exponential, with a mean of 10 minutes and an SD of 10. Lots of short waits and a long tail of rare long ones, so it is clearly skewed to the right.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(99)\n\ndef sample_means(n, reps=5000):\n    return rng.exponential(10, size=(reps, n)).mean(axis=1)\n\nsingle = rng.exponential(10, size=5000)\nprint(f'skewness of single waits = {stats.skew(single):.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arithmetic and random numbers.","Load matplotlib; we draw histograms in a later step.","Load SciPy's stats tools, which include skewness and the normal distribution.","Create a seeded random generator (seed 99) so the results are repeatable.","Define a helper function that takes a sample size n (and a number of repeats) and returns the sample means.","Inside it, draw reps samples of n exponential waits (mean 10) and average each row; return gives that list back.","Draw 5,000 single waits (n = 1) so we can look at the raw skewed shape.","Print the skewness of single waits; skewness is 0 for a symmetric shape and positive when there is a long right tail."],"meaning":"The skewness of single waiting times is 1.89, far above 0. Individual waits are strongly skewed to the right: many short waits and a few very long ones. That is why a bell curve cannot describe a single wait.","out":"skewness of single waits = 1.89","figs":[]},{"title":"Skewness of the sample means","text":"Skewness is 0 for a perfectly symmetric shape. For each n we take 5,000 sample means and measure their skewness. It falls towards 0 roughly like 2 divided by the square root of n.","code":"print('  n   skewness of means   2/sqrt(n)')\nfor n in (1, 5, 30):\n    m = sample_means(n)\n    print(f'{n:3d}   {stats.skew(m):15.2f}   {2 / np.sqrt(n):9.2f}')","explain":["Print column headings for the table.","Loop over three sample sizes: single waits, means of 5, and means of 30.","Use the helper to get 5,000 sample means of size n.","Print n, the skewness of those means, and the rough rule 2/sqrt(n) for comparison."],"meaning":"Skewness falls as n grows: 1.93 for n = 1, 0.85 for n = 5, and 0.33 for n = 30, close to the rule of thumb 2/sqrt(n) (2.00, 0.89, 0.37). Averaging more readings makes the distribution of means more and more symmetric.","out":"  n   skewness of means   2/sqrt(n)\n  1              1.93        2.00\n  5              0.85        0.89\n 30              0.33        0.37","figs":[]},{"title":"Three histograms","text":"The same story as a picture. With n = 1 we see the skewed population itself. With n = 5 the shape is already more balanced, and with n = 30 it looks close to a bell.","code":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(6, 3.4), sharex=True)\nfor ax, n in zip(axes, (1, 5, 30)):\n    ax.hist(sample_means(n), bins=np.linspace(0, 40, 41), color='steelblue', edgecolor='white')\n    ax.set_title(f'n = {n}')\n    ax.set_yticks([])\naxes[1].set_xlabel('sample mean (minutes)')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","explain":["Make a row of three small plots that share the same x-axis, so the shapes are directly comparable.","Loop over each plot together with its sample size.","Draw a histogram of 5,000 sample means, 40 equal-width bars from 0 to 40 minutes.","Title each plot with its n.","Hide the vertical tick labels; only the shape matters here.","Label the shared horizontal axis once, on the middle plot.","Tidy the spacing so labels do not overlap.","Display the figure."],"meaning":"With n = 1 the histogram is the skewed pile of raw waits. With n = 5 it is more balanced but still leans right. With n = 30 it is close to a symmetric bell centred near 10 minutes. This is the Central Limit Theorem in action.","out":"","figs":["4.3-3.png"]},{"title":"Use the bell curve","text":"The lesson's clinic question: for n = 40 patients, how often does the mean wait reach 12 minutes or more? The CLT lets us use a normal curve with mean 10 and SE = 10 divided by the square root of 40.","code":"se = 10 / np.sqrt(40)\np_clt = stats.norm.sf(12, loc=10, scale=se)\nsimulated = (sample_means(40, reps=20000) >= 12).mean()\n\nprint(f'SE = {se:.3f}')\nprint(f'CLT answer       P(mean >= 12) = {p_clt:.3f}')\nprint(f'simulation (20k) P(mean >= 12) = {simulated:.3f}')","explain":["Standard error for 40 patients: SD 10 divided by the square root of 40.","Use the bell curve: stats.norm.sf gives the chance of a mean at or above 12 with centre 10 and spread equal to the SE.","Check by brute force: simulate 20,000 audits of 40 patients and count the share with a mean of 12 or more.","Print the standard error.","Print the answer from the bell curve.","Print the answer from the simulation."],"meaning":"The standard error is 1.581. The bell curve says the chance that 40 patients average 12 minutes or more is 0.103; the simulation gives 0.110, nearly the same. So an average of 12 happens about 1 time in 10 by chance alone, which is not strong evidence of a real change.","out":"SE = 1.581\nCLT answer       P(mean >= 12) = 0.103\nsimulation (20k) P(mean >= 12) = 0.110","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"skew <- function(x) mean((x - mean(x))^3) / mean((x - mean(x))^2)^1.5\nset.seed(99)\nfor (n in c(1, 5, 30)) print(skew(replicate(5000, mean(rexp(n, rate = 1/10)))))\npnorm(12, mean = 10, sd = 10 / sqrt(40), lower.tail = FALSE)","expect":"skewness about 1.9, 0.9, 0.35 for n = 1, 5, 30 (Python: 1.93, 0.85, 0.33); pnorm answer 0.1029","note":"Base R has no skewness function, so we write the same formula SciPy uses. Simulated skewness values are close to Python's, not identical; the pnorm answer is exact.","explain":["Define a skewness function using the same formula SciPy uses, since base R has none.","Fix the random seed so the run can be repeated.","For n = 1, 5 and 30, simulate 5,000 sample means of exponential waits (mean 10) and print their skewness.","Compute the chance of a mean at or above 12 using the normal curve (lower.tail = FALSE means the upper tail)."],"read":"The loop prints three skewness values near 1.9, 0.9 and 0.35, falling as n grows (Python: 1.93, 0.85, 0.33). The pnorm line prints 0.1029, the chance that a mean of 40 waits is 12 or more."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives > Options > Skewness","syntax":"SET SEED=99.\nINPUT PROGRAM.\nLOOP s = 1 TO 5000.\nCOMPUTE x1 = RV.EXP(1/10).\nCOMPUTE total = 0.\nLOOP i = 1 TO 30.\nCOMPUTE total = total + RV.EXP(1/10).\nEND LOOP.\nCOMPUTE mean30 = total/30.\nEND CASE.\nEND LOOP.\nEND FILE.\nEND INPUT PROGRAM.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=x1 mean30 /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV SKEWNESS.","note":"x1 is a single wait (n = 1) and mean30 is a mean of 30 waits, so you can compare their skewness. SPSS uses a slightly adjusted skewness formula and its own random numbers, so values are close to Python's, not identical.","explain":["Fix the random seed so the run can be repeated.","INPUT PROGRAM lets us build a data set from nothing.","Outer loop: one pass for each of 5,000 simulated clinics.","x1 is a single exponential wait with mean 10 (rate 1/10).","Start the running total at zero.","Inner loop: 30 waits.","Add one wait to the total.","Close the inner loop.","mean30 is the mean of those 30 waits.","END CASE writes one row.","Close the outer loop.","Tell SPSS the data set is complete.","End the input program.","Ask for the mean, SD and skewness of both a single wait and a mean of 30."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table compare the Skewness column: x1 should be around 2 and mean30 much smaller, around 0.4. Both means are near 10. SPSS uses a slightly adjusted skewness formula and its own random numbers."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset seed 99\nset obs 5000\ngenerate x1 = rexponential(10)\ngenerate mean30 = 0\nforvalues i = 1/30 {\n    quietly replace mean30 = mean30 + rexponential(10) / 30\n}\ntabstat x1 mean30, statistics(mean sd skewness)\ndisplay 1 - normal((12 - 10) / (10 / sqrt(40)))","note":"Stata's skewness uses the same formula as SciPy. Simulated values are close to Python's but not identical; the last line gives the exact normal answer of 0.103.","explain":["Clear any data in memory.","Fix the random seed so the run can be repeated.","Create 5,000 empty rows.","x1 is one exponential wait with mean 10 per row (n = 1).","Start mean30 at 0; we will add to it.","Repeat the next step 30 times.","Add one fresh wait divided by 30, so after 30 passes mean30 is the mean of 30 waits.","Close the loop.","Print mean, SD and skewness for both variables so we can compare them.","Compute the chance of a mean of 12 or more with the normal curve: 1 minus normal(z) where z = (12-10)/(10/sqrt(40))."],"read":"In the tabstat table, skewness of x1 should be around 2 and of mean30 much smaller (about 0.35). Both means are near 10 and the SD of mean30 is near 1.8. The final display shows 0.1029, the normal-curve answer."},"conclusion":"Single waits are strongly skewed (skewness 1.89), but means of 30 are nearly symmetric (0.33), so the bell curve works for averages. With 40 patients the chance of an average wait of 12 or more is about 0.10 (0.103 by the bell curve, 0.110 by simulation), so 12 minutes is not unusual. Caution: n around 30 is a rule of thumb; very skewed data may need more.","practice":{"scenario":"An IT help desk finds that tickets take on average 20 minutes to resolve, with an SD of 20 minutes, and the times are very skewed. A manager looks at a random batch of 36 tickets and wants to know how often such a batch averages 24 minutes or more just by chance.","data":"No list needed: model single ticket times as rng.exponential(20, ...) (mean 20, SD 20) with rng = np.random.default_rng(5), and use batches of n = 36.","tasks":["Find the standard error of the mean of 36 tickets.","Use the normal curve to find P(mean >= 24).","Check by simulating 20,000 batches of 36 tickets.","Measure the skewness of the simulated batch means."],"hint":"SE = SD / sqrt(n); stats.norm.sf(24, loc=20, scale=se) gives the upper tail. For the simulation, draw a (20000, 36) array of exponentials and average each row.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(5)\nse = 20 / np.sqrt(36)\np_clt = stats.norm.sf(24, loc=20, scale=se)\nmeans = rng.exponential(20, size=(20_000, 36)).mean(axis=1)\nprint(f'SE = {se:.2f}')\nprint(f'CLT answer P(mean >= 24) = {p_clt:.3f}')\nprint(f'simulation P(mean >= 24) = {(means >= 24).mean():.3f}')\nprint(f'skewness of the means = {stats.skew(means):.2f}')","answer":"The standard error is 3.33 minutes. The normal curve gives P(mean >= 24) = 0.115 and the simulation gives 0.120, so about 1 batch in 9 would average 24 minutes or more by chance. The batch means have skewness 0.34, far less than the very skewed single tickets, which is why the bell curve works.","out":"SE = 3.33\nCLT answer P(mean >= 24) = 0.115\nsimulation P(mean >= 24) = 0.120\nskewness of the means = 0.34","figs":[]}}});
